            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the running total of the area under a curve: at each sample, the integral from the start up to that point. It starts at 0 and has one value per sample, so it lines up with the input.\n\nFrom speeds it gives the distance travelled so far - the position at each moment; from accelerations, the speed so far; from a joint's power, the work done so far. Each step adds the trapezoid between two neighbouring samples: their average times the spacing.\n\ntorch's own cumulative_trapezoid leaves out the starting 0 and so is one shorter; this node puts it back, as np.cumulative_trapezoid does. The answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.cumulative_trapezoid <spacing: float>\nDefault: spacing 1.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe samples (a list or tensor). Every input is integrated and sent.\n\nspacing (float box, also an inlet):\nThe distance between samples - the time between frames (1/60 for 60 frames a second), so the totals come out per second.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to integrate: -1 (the default) along each row, 0 down each column - one running total per joint for a buffer of frames x joints.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrunning total:\nThe area so far at each sample, starting from 0, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA speed rising steadily from 0 to 4 metres a second, sampled every half second. The distance so far is 0, 0.25, 1, 2.25 and 4 metres: growing faster and faster as the speed builds - the squares, as a steady acceleration gives. The last value is t.trapezoid's total.\n\nRELATED:\nt.trapezoid gives only the final total. t.cumsum is the running sum without the averaging. t.gradient goes the other way, from the running total back to the rate. np.cumulative_trapezoid is the numpy version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 960,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the running total of the area under a curve: at each sample, the integral from the start up to that point. It starts at 0 and has one value per sample, so it lines up with the input.\n\nFrom speeds it gives the distance travelled so far - the position at each moment; from accelerations, the speed so far; from a joint's power, the work done so far. Each step adds the trapezoid between two neighbouring samples: their average times the spacing.\n\ntorch's own cumulative_trapezoid leaves out the starting 0 and so is one shorter; this node puts it back, as np.cumulative_trapezoid does. The answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.cumulative_trapezoid <spacing: float>\nDefault: spacing 1.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe samples (a list or tensor). Every input is integrated and sent.\n\nspacing (float box, also an inlet):\nThe distance between samples - the time between frames (1/60 for 60 frames a second), so the totals come out per second.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to integrate: -1 (the default) along each row, 0 down each column - one running total per joint for a buffer of frames x joints.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrunning total:\nThe area so far at each sample, starting from 0, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA speed rising steadily from 0 to 4 metres a second, sampled every half second. The distance so far is 0, 0.25, 1, 2.25 and 4 metres: growing faster and faster as the speed builds - the squares, as a steady acceleration gives. The last value is t.trapezoid's total.\n\nRELATED:\nt.trapezoid gives only the final total. t.cumsum is the running sum without the averaging. t.gradient goes the other way, from the running total back to the rate. np.cumulative_trapezoid is the numpy version."
        },
